.Dd July 06, 2024
.Dt AGE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm age
.Nd file encryption format with multiple pluggable recipients
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Ss Conventions used in this document
ABNF syntax follows RFC 5234 and RFC 7405 and references the core rules
in RFC 5234, Appendix\~B.1.
.Pp
The base64 encoding used throughout is the standard Base 64 encoding
specified in RFC 4648, Section\~4, without
.Ql =
padding characters (sometimes referred to as
.Dq raw
or
.Dq unpadded
base64).
Encoders MUST generate canonical base64 according to RFC 4648,
Section\~3.5, and decoders MUST reject non-canonical encodings and
encodings ending with
.Ql =
padding characters.
.Pp
Keys derived with HKDF-SHA-256 are produced by applying HKDF-Extract
with the specified salt followed by HKDF-Expand with the specified
info according to RFC 5869.
The hash used with HKDF in this specification is always SHA-256.
The length of the output keying material is always 32 bytes.
.Pp
ChaCha20-Poly1305 is the AEAD encryption function from RFC 7539.
.Pp
.Ql ||
denotes concatenation.
.Ql 0x
followed by two hexadecimal characters denotes a byte value in the
0-255 range.
.Pp
The key words
.Dq MUST ,
.Dq MUST NOT ,
.Dq REQUIRED ,
.Dq SHALL ,
.Dq SHALL NOT ,
.Dq SHOULD ,
.Dq SHOULD NOT ,
.Dq RECOMMENDED ,
.Dq NOT RECOMMENDED ,
.Dq MAY
and
.Dq OPTIONAL
in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14, RFC 2119,
RFC 8174 when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown
here.
.Ss Encrypted file format
.Pp
An
.Nm
file is composed of two parts: a textual
.Sx Header
that carries the
.Em file key ,
and a binary
.Sx Payload
encrypted with it.
Overall,
.Nm
files MUST be treated as binary, and are not malleable without
knowledge of the file key.
.Pp
.Nm
files MAY use the extension
.Ql .age ,
in both their binary and armored
.Sx ASCII armor
formats.
.Ss File key
Each file is encrypted with a 128-bit symmetric
.Em file key .
The
.Em file key
MUST be generated as 16 bytes of CSPRNG output.
It MUST NOT be reused across multiple files.
.Ss Header
The textual file header wraps the
.Em file key
for one or more
.Em recipients ,
so that it can be unwrapped by one of the corresponding
.Em identities .
It starts with a version line, followed by one or more recipient
stanzas, and ends with a MAC.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
age-encryption.org/v1
-> X25519 XEl0dJ6y3C7KZkgmgWUicg63EyXJiwBJW8PdYJ/cYBE
qRS0AMjdjPvZ/WT08U2KL4G+PIooA3hy38SvLpvaC1E
--- HK2NmOBN9Dpq0Gw6xMCuhFcQlQLvZ/wQUi/2scLG75s
.Ed
.Pp
Note that each section of the header can be parsed by looking at its
first three characters, and it ends either at the next newline (for
version and MAC lines) or at the first line shorter than 64 columns (for
stanzas).
.Ss Version line
The version line always starts with
.Ql age-encryption.org/ ,
is followed by an arbitrary version string, and ends with a line feed
.Po
.Ql 0x0A
.Pc .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
version-line = %s"age-encryption.org/" version LF

version = 1*VCHAR
.Ed
.Pp
This document only specifies the
.Ql v1
format.
Anything after the end of the version line may change in future
versions.
.Ss Recipient stanza
A recipient stanza starts with
.Ql -> ,
followed after a space by one or more space-separated arguments, and a
base64-encoded body wrapped at 64 columns.
The body MUST end with a line shorter than 64 characters, which MAY
be empty.
.Pp
Each recipient stanza wraps the same
.Em file key
independently.
Identity implementations are provided the full set of stanzas and
recognize those addressed to them from their arguments.
Identity implementations MUST ignore unrecognized stanzas, unless they
wish to require that the recipient type they implement is not mixed
with other types.
.Pp
It is RECOMMENDED that non-native recipient implementations use
fully-qualified names as the first stanza argument, such as
.Ql example.com/enigma ,
to avoid ambiguity and conflicts.
.Pp
Recipient implementations MAY choose to include an identifier of the
specific recipient (for example, a short hash of the public key) as an
argument.
Note that this sacrifices any chance of ciphertext anonymity and
unlinkability.
.Ss Header Mac
The final header line starts with
.Ql ---
and is followed after a space by the base64-encoded MAC of the header.
The MAC is computed with HMAC-SHA-256 (see RFC 2104) over the whole
header up to and including the
.Ql ---
mark (excluding the space following it).
.Pp
The HMAC key is computed as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
HMAC key = HKDF-SHA-256(ikm = file key, salt = empty,
    info = "header")
.Ed
.Ss ABNF definition of file header
The following is the ABNF definition of the v1 file header.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
header = v1-line 1*stanza end

v1-line = %s"age-encryption.org/v1" LF

end = \(dq--- \(dq 43base64char LF

base64char = ALPHA / DIGIT / "+" / "/"

stanza = arg-line *full-line final-line

arg-line = "-> " argument *(SP argument) LF

argument = 1*VCHAR

full-line = 64base64char LF

final-line = *63base64char LF
.Ed
.Ss Payload
The binary payload encrypts the file body and starts immediately after
the header.
It begins with a 16-byte nonce generated by the sender from a CSPRNG.
A new nonce MUST be generated for each file.
.Pp
The payload key is computed as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
payload key = HKDF-SHA-256(ikm = file key, salt = nonce,
    info = "payload")
.Ed
.Pp
The payload is split in chunks of 64 KiB, and each of them is encrypted
with ChaCha20-Poly1305, using the payload key and a 12-byte nonce
composed as follows: the first 11 bytes are a big endian chunk counter
starting at zero and incrementing by one for each subsequent chunk;
the last byte is 0x01 for the final chunk and 0x00 for all preceding
ones.
The final chunk MAY be shorter than 64 KiB but MUST NOT be empty
unless the whole payload is empty.
.Pp
This is a STREAM variant from
.Lk https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/189 "Online Authenticated-Encryption and its Nonce-Reuse Misuse-Resistance" .
It is similar to those used by
.Lk https://github.com/google/tink/blob/59bb34495d1cb8f9d9dbc0f0a52c4f9e21491a14/docs/WIRE-FORMAT.md#streaming-encryption "Tink"
and
.Lk https://github.com/miscreant/meta/wiki/STREAM Miscreant ,
but it doesn't prefix the AEAD nonce with key material as the payload
key is 256 bits (enough even to provide a security margin in the
multi-target setting) and derived from both file key and nonce.
.Pp
The payload can be streamed by decrypting or encrypting one chunk at a
time.
Streaming decryption MUST signal an error if the end of file is
reached without successfully decrypting a final chunk.
.Pp
The payload can be seeked by jumping ahead in chunk increments, and
decrypting the whole chunk that contains the seeked position.
Seeking relatively to the end of file MUST first decrypt and verify
that the last chunk is a valid final chunk.
.Pp
The payload MUST NOT be modified without re-encrypting it as a new file
with a fresh nonce.
.Ss Native recipient types
This document specifies two core
.Nm
recipient types: an asymmetric encryption type based on X25519,
and a passphrase encryption type based on scrypt.
.Ss The X25519 recipient type
An X25519 identity is generated as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
identity = read(CSPRNG, 32)
.Ed
.Pp
and encoded as
.Lk https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0173.mediawiki Bech32
with HRP
.Ql AGE-SECRET-KEY-
.Bd -literal -offset
AGE-SECRET-KEY-1GFPYYSJZGFPYYSJZGFPYYSJZGFPYYSJZGFPYYSJZGFPYYSJZGFPQ4EGAEX
.Ed
.Pp
The corresponding recipient is computed as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
recipient = X25519(identity, basepoint)
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Ql X25519
is from RFC 7748, Section 5 and
.Ql basepoint
is the Curve25519 base point from RFC 7748, Section 4.1.
.Pp
The recipient is encoded as Bech32 with HRP
.Ql age .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
age1zvkyg2lqzraa2lnjvqej32nkuu0ues2s82hzrye869xeexvn73equnujwj
.Ed
.Pp
Note that Bech32 strings can only be all uppercase or all lowercase, but
the checksum is always computed over the lowercase string.
.Ss X25519 recipient stanza
An X25519 recipient stanza has two arguments.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-> X25519 O6DLx/wDIawpUC978NSPjYvrfDtJVnZApXKp4FMPHCY
3aKjozt9agh7jGmvOKvR4iax41Wl4zj95MKK4X9JuWc
.Ed
.Pp
The first is the fixed string
.Ql X25519
and the second is the base64-encoded ephemeral share computed by the
recipient implementation as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ephemeral secret = read(CSPRNG, 32)
ephemeral share = X25519(ephemeral secret, basepoint)
.Ed
.Pp
A new ephemeral secret MUST be generated for each stanza and each file.
.Pp
The body of the recipient stanza is computed by the recipient
implementation as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
salt = ephemeral share || recipient
info = "age-encryption.org/v1/X25519"
shared secret = X25519(ephemeral secret, recipient)
wrap key = HKDF-SHA-256(ikm = shared secret, salt, info)
body = ChaCha20-Poly1305(key = wrap key, plaintext = file key)
.Ed
.Pp
where the ChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce is fixed as 12 0x00 bytes.
.Pp
The identity implementation MUST ignore any stanza that does not have
.Ql X25519
as the first argument, and MUST otherwise reject any stanza that has
more or less than two arguments, or where the second argument is not a
canonical encoding of a 32-byte value.
It MUST check that the body length is exactly 32 bytes before
attempting to decrypt it.
.Pp
The identity implementation computes the shared secret as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
shared secret = X25519(identity, ephemeral share)
.Ed
.Pp
If the shared secret is all 0x00 bytes, the identity implementation
MUST abort.
.Pp
Finally, it derives the key as above and decrypts the file key in the
body.
.Ss The scrypt recipient type
The scrypt recipient and identity implementations encrypt and decrypt
the file key with a provided passphrase.
.Ss scrypt recipient stanza
An scrypt recipient stanza has three arguments.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-> scrypt ajMFur+EJLGaohv/dLRGnw 18
8SHBz/ldWnjyGFQqfjat6uNBarWqqEMDS7W8X7+Xq5Q
.Ed
.Pp
The first is the string
.Ql scrypt ,
the second is a base64-encoded salt computed by the recipient
implementation as 16 bytes from a CSPRNG, and the third is the base-two
logarithm of the scrypt work factor in decimal.
.Pp
A new salt MUST be generated for each stanza and each file.
.Pp
The body is computed as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wrap key = scrypt(N = work factor, r = 8, p = 1, dkLen = 32,
    S = "age-encryption.org/v1/scrypt" || salt, P = passphrase)
body = ChaCha20-Poly1305(key = wrap key, plaintext = file key)
.Ed
.Pp
where the ChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce is fixed as 12 0x00 bytes and scrypt
is from RFC 7914.
.Pp
The identity implementation MUST reject any scrypt stanza that has more
or less than three arguments, where the second argument is not a
canonical encoding of a 16-byte value, or where the third argument is
not a decimal number composed of only digits with no leading zeroes
.Po
.Ql %x31-39 *DIGIT
in ABNF or
.Ql ^[1-9][0-9]*$
in regular expression
.Pc .
The identity implementation SHOULD apply an
upper limit to the work factor, and it MUST check that the body length
is exactly 32 bytes before attempting to decrypt it.
.Pp
An scrypt stanza, if present, MUST be the only stanza in the header.
In other words, scrypt stanzas MAY NOT be mixed with other scrypt
stanzas or stanzas of other types.
This is to uphold an expectation of authentication that is implicit
in password-based encryption.
The identity implementation MUST reject headers where an scrypt
stanza is present alongside any other stanza.
.Ss ASCII armor
.Nm
files that need to be transmitted as 7-bit ASCII SHOULD be encoded
according to the strict PEM encoding specified in RFC 7468, Section\~3
(Figure\~3), with case-sensitive label
.Ql AGE ENCRYPTED FILE .
Note that this encoding employs base64 with
.Ql =
padding characters, unlike the rest of this document.
.Pp
Note that ASCII armored files are malleable unless care is taken to
reject any data before and after the PEM encoding, a strict PEM parser
is used, and canonical base64 is enforced.
.Nm
implementations SHOULD reject non-canonical ASCII armor encodings
except for whitespace before and after the PEM block, and MAY choose
to accept both LF and CRLF line endings.
.Ss Test vectors
A comprehensive set of test vectors is avaliable at
.Lk https://age-encryption.org/testkit .
